  "worked_solution": "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4\\sqrt{t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{7}{3}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{8}{3}(1)^{3/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{8}{3}+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 8{t}^{3/2} & =1 \\\\ {t}^{3/2} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ t & ={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2/3}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{s}.$",
